window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-1", "text": "Find the trace norm and operator norm of diag(3,−2). The singular values are 3 and 2, giving trace norm 5 and operator norm 3."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-2", "text": "Find \\(T\\) between diag(0.7,0.3) and diag(0.2,0.8). The difference has eigenvalues ±0.5. Its trace norm is 1, so \\(T=0.5\\). Equal-prior success is 0.75."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-3", "text": "If two candidate states coincide and their priors are 0.8 and 0.2, what is optimal? Always guess the more likely state; success is 0.8. The weighted difference formula gives the same result."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute fidelity between |0⟩ and diag(0.6,0.4). \\(F=\\langle0|\\sigma|0\\rangle=0.6\\); root fidelity is \\(\\sqrt{0.6}\\)."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-5", "text": "Find \\(T\\) between two pure states with overlap magnitude 0.6. \\(F=0.36\\), \\(T=\\sqrt{0.64}=0.8\\), and equal-prior success is 0.9."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-6", "text": "Construct an optimal effect for diagonal states diag(0.7,0.3) and diag(0.2,0.8). \\(M=|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\). Success is \\((0.7+0.8)/2=0.75\\)."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-7", "text": "For the chapter’s asymmetric example, what happens when ε=0? Acceptance must be certain on both outcomes, so M=I and β=1. Zero misses are expensive here."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does fidelity zero imply identical states? No. Fidelity zero means orthogonal supports; identical normalized states have fidelity one."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-9", "text": "Can trace distance increase after conditioning on a rare measurement outcome? Yes. Postselection is nonlinear after normalization; contraction applies to the full trace-preserving process, including its success and failure record."}, {"title": "Distinguishability and Testing — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#exercise-10", "text": "Prove that mixing a fixed state into both candidates contracts their distance by exactly 1−t. For \\(\\rho_t=(1-t)\\rho+t\\tau\\) and \\(\\sigma_t=(1-t)\\sigma+t\\tau\\), their difference is \\((1-t)(\\rho-\\sigma)\\). Norm homogeneity gives \\(T(\\rho_t,\\sigma_t)=(1-t)T(\\rho,\\sigma)\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le1\\)."}, {"title": "From matrices to decisions", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 From matrices to decisions 1.1.1 The trace norm as a measurement advantage Let us begin with a fair choice: each preparation occurs with probability one half. Measure in the computational basis \\(\\{|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle\\}\\). If the preparation was \\(|0\\rangle\\), you certainly observe 0. If it was \\(|+\\rangle\\), you observe 0 or 1 with equal probability. An outcome of 1 therefore identifies \\(|+\\rangle\\) without ambiguity. An outcome of 0 leaves both preparations possible. What should we guess when we see 0? Across many trials, half of all trials start with \\(|0\\rangle\\) and produce 0. Another quarter start with \\(|+\\rangle\\) and produce 0. Among the trials giving this outcome, \\(|0\\rangle\\) is twice as common. So our decision rule should be: guess \\(|0\\rangle\\) on outcome 0, and \\(|+\\rangle\\) on outcome 1. Its success probability is \\[p_{\\mathrm{success}}=\\frac12\\underbrace{(1)}_{\\text{correct given }|0\\rangle}+\\frac12\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac12\\right)}_{\\text{correct given }|+\\rangle}=\\frac34.\\] We have found a strategy that works 75% of the time. Have we found the best strategy? Nothing in this calculation establishes that. It only evaluates the measurement we happened to choose. A different measurement basis could trade some reliability on the first preparation for a larger improvement on the second. To find out, we need a way to describe all allowed decisions, not just computational-basis measurements. We will eventually express the best success probability using a quantity called the trace norm. Rather than introducing it as a definition to memorize, let us first do the optimization that makes it necessary. 1.1.2 Writing down every possible decision Replace our two particular pure states by arbitrary density operators \\(\\rho\\) and \\(\\sigma\\). The source chooses \\(\\rho\\) with probability \\(p\\) and \\(\\sigma\\) with probability \\(1-p\\). These are the prior probabilities : what we know before looking at the system. We receive one system, know the two candidate states and the prior, and must guess one of them. There is no “inconclusive” answer in this task. Even if a measuring apparatus has many possible outcomes, our final answer has only two. We can group all outcomes leading to the guess “\\(\\rho\\)” into one measurement effect M; the remaining outcomes form \\(I-M\\). Randomized decision rules can also be included in these effects. By the probability rule, \\[\\Pr(\\text{guess }\\rho\\mid\\rho)=\\tr(M\\rho),\\qquad \\Pr(\\text{guess }\\sigma\\mid\\sigma)=\\tr[(I-M)\\sigma].\\] The allowed effects satisfy \\(0\\le M\\le I\\). These are operator inequalities: M and I−M must both be positive semidefinite. Equivalently, every eigenvalue of M lies between zero and one. This guarantees valid probabilities for every input state. M need not be a projector; restricting it to projectors at this stage would require justification. Weighting the two ways to be correct by their priors gives \\[\\begin{aligned}p_{\\mathrm{success}}(M)&=p\\tr(M\\rho)+(1-p)\\tr[(I-M)\\sigma]\\\\&=1-p+\\tr(MH),\\qquad H=p\\rho-(1-p)\\sigma.\\end{aligned}\\] Now the task is precise. The source fixes H. We choose M, subject to \\(0\\le M\\le I\\), to make \\(\\tr(MH)\\) as large as possible. The constant 1−p is the success probability of always guessing \\(\\sigma\\); the remaining term measures what our decision rule adds to that strategy. 1.1.3 Finding the best measurement H is Hermitian, so choose an orthonormal eigenbasis and write \\(H=\\sum_i h_i|i\\rangle\\langle i|\\). In this basis, \\[\\tr(MH)=\\sum_i h_i\\langle i|M|i\\rangle.\\] Look at one term. If \\(h_i\\) is positive, we want its coefficient \\(\\langle i|M|i\\rangle\\) as large as possible: one. If \\(h_i\\) is negative, we want that coefficient to be zero. The constraints on M allow neither values above one nor values below zero. Therefore every allowed effect obeys \\[\\tr(MH)\\le\\sum_{h_i>0}h_i.\\] An upper bound is only half of an optimality proof. Can a single physical effect attain all these choices simultaneously? Yes: choose \\(M_*\\) to be the projector onto the positive-eigenvalue subspace of H. Its diagonal entries are exactly the zeros and ones we need, and it is a valid effect. Zero eigenvalues do not affect the score, so the choice there is immaterial. We have both a bound that applies to every measurement and a measurement that reaches it. To put the result in a compact form, let \\(a\\) be the sum of positive eigenvalues of H and \\(b\\) the sum of the absolute values of its negative eigenvalues. Then \\(\\tr H=a-b=2p-1\\). The trace norm of a Hermitian operator is the sum of its absolute eigenvalues, so \\(\\|H\\|_1=a+b\\). Adding these relations gives \\(a=(\\|H\\|_1+2p-1)/2\\). Substituting into our success probability yields \\[\\boxed{p_{\\mathrm{guess}}=\\max_{0\\le M\\le I}p_{\\mathrm{success}}(M)=\\frac{1+\\|p\\rho-(1-p)\\sigma\\|_1}{2}.}\\] This is the binary minimum-error discrimination formula, often called the Helstrom bound. Here we have derived it, including the optimizing measurement. For a general matrix X, the trace norm is \\(\\|X\\|_1=\\tr\\sqrt{X^\\dagger X}\\), the sum of its singular values. For Hermitian H, those singular values are \\(|h_i|\\), which is why that norm appeared in our calculation. The operator norm \\(\\|X\\|_\\infty\\) instead keeps only the largest singular value. These norms answer different questions. With equal priors, the formula becomes particularly transparent. Define the trace distance by \\[T(\\rho,\\sigma)=\\frac12\\|\\rho-\\sigma\\|_1,\\qquad p_{\\mathrm{guess}}=\\frac{1+T(\\rho,\\sigma)}2.\\] The factor one half normalizes T to lie between zero and one. Identical states give T=0 and no improvement over guessing. States with orthogonal supports give T=1 and can be identified perfectly. For intermediate values, T tells us exactly how much distinguishability the best one-shot measurement can extract: the success advantage over a random guess is T/2. 1.1.4 Returning to the qubit Now we can settle the question we left open. In the ordered basis \\((|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle)\\), our original states and their difference are \\[\\rho=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad D=\\rho-\\sigma=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\-1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Multiplying D by itself gives \\(D^2=I/2\\). Its eigenvalues therefore have magnitude \\(1/\\sqrt2\\); because its trace is zero, one is positive and the other negative. Thus \\(\\|D\\|_1=\\sqrt2\\), and \\[T=\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\qquad p_{\\mathrm{guess}}=\\frac{1+1/\\sqrt2}{2}\\approx0.853553.\\] Our 75% strategy was useful, but it was not optimal. The positive-eigenvalue eigenvector is \\(|m_\\rho\\rangle=\\cos(\\pi/8)|0\\rangle-\\sin(\\pi/8)|1\\rangle\\). Measure in the orthonormal basis containing this vector, and guess \\(\\rho\\) when it clicks. For \\(|0\\rangle\\), that outcome occurs with probability \\(\\cos^2(\\pi/8)\\). For \\(|+\\rangle\\), the same outcome occurs with probability \\(\\sin^2(\\pi/8)\\), so the complementary outcome correctly identifies \\(|+\\rangle\\) with probability \\(\\cos^2(\\pi/8)\\). Both conditional success probabilities are approximately 0.853553. The improved measurement gives up certainty on \\(|0\\rangle\\) to gain more on \\(|+\\rangle\\). The optimum still falls short of one. That remaining error is not a limitation of our chosen basis or experimental patience: the bound covers every allowed measurement on this one supplied system. Worked check 1.1. Suppose the source instead gives the same state under both labels, but chooses label \\(\\rho\\) with probability 0.8. What should you do? Check your answer using the bound. Reasoned solution The system carries no evidence about the label, so always guess the more likely label. This succeeds with probability 0.8. Algebraically, \\(H=0.8\\rho-0.2\\rho=0.6\\rho\\). Positivity and unit trace give \\(\\|H\\|_1=0.6\\), hence \\(p_{\\mathrm{guess}}=(1+0.6)/2=0.8\\). Unequal priors can make a good guess possible even when the states are identical."}, {"title": "Fidelity and physical processing", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Fidelity and physical processing 1.2.1 Why discarding cannot improve a test Suppose we pass the supplied state through a noisy device before trying to identify it. Could the device make the task easier? It might improve the performance of a particular detector. But could it improve on the best possible measurement of the original input? Think of the entire procedure—device followed by measurement—as one larger measuring apparatus. It is still a valid experiment on the original system. Our earlier optimization already allowed every such experiment. Therefore a fixed physical process cannot increase the optimal success probability. With equal priors, the relation between success and trace distance immediately gives \\[T(\\mathcal N(\\rho),\\mathcal N(\\sigma))\\le T(\\rho,\\sigma),\\] where \\(\\mathcal N\\) denotes the same quantum channel applied to either candidate. A channel is a deterministic physical operation on the system, possibly involving an ancilla and discarding part of the output. Chapter 2 will develop its mathematical descriptions. We can also make the apparatus argument explicit. If M is an output effect, its input effect is \\(\\mathcal N^\\dagger(M)\\), where the adjoint is defined by \\(\\tr[M\\mathcal N(\\tau)]=\\tr[\\mathcal N^\\dagger(M)\\tau]\\). A channel’s adjoint is positive and sends I to I. Consequently \\(0\\le M\\le I\\) implies \\(0\\le\\mathcal N^\\dagger(M)\\le I\\). Every output decision therefore corresponds to an allowed input decision, although the reverse need not hold. The deterministic qualification matters. If we keep only a rare successful measurement outcome and discard the other trials, the surviving conditional states can become easier to distinguish. The success probability of that selection is then part of the accounting. The contraction inequality applies to the complete trace-preserving process, not to a selected branch after renormalization. 1.2.2 Overlap as a second comparison Trace distance asks how well we can tell two states apart. Sometimes our question is framed the other way around: how well does a prepared state agree with a target? If the target is a pure state \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\), a natural test measures the projector \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|\\). An actual state \\(\\sigma\\) passes with probability \\(\\langle\\psi|\\sigma|\\psi\\rangle\\). This motivates a second quantity, fidelity . For general mixed states we use \\[F(\\rho,\\sigma)=\\left\\|\\sqrt\\rho\\sqrt\\sigma\\right\\|_1^2.\\] The square root of a positive matrix is obtained by taking square roots of its eigenvalues while keeping its eigenvectors. If \\(\\rho=|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|\\), its square root is itself. The product \\(\\sqrt\\rho\\sqrt\\sigma\\) then has only one possibly nonzero singular value, equal to \\(\\sqrt{\\langle\\psi|\\sigma|\\psi\\rangle}\\). Squaring its trace norm recovers the test probability above. For mixed targets the formula remains useful, but should not be interpreted as the pass probability of that same pure-state projector test. We use squared fidelity throughout this course. Some references call \\(\\sqrt F\\) fidelity instead. Neither convention changes the physics, but mixing them changes equations. For two pure states, our convention gives \\(F=|\\langle\\psi|\\phi\\rangle|^2\\). Identical states have fidelity one; orthogonal states have fidelity zero. Larger fidelity means more similarity, whereas larger trace distance means more distinguishability. 1.2.3 Connecting fidelity to distinguishability For two pure states, we can derive the relationship directly. Choose their phases so that \\(\\langle\\psi|\\phi\\rangle=c\\) is real and nonnegative. For \\(c<1\\), choose a unit vector \\(|\\eta\\rangle\\) orthogonal to \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\) such that \\(|\\phi\\rangle=c|\\psi\\rangle+\\sqrt{1-c^2}|\\eta\\rangle\\). In this two-dimensional span, the difference of their density matrices is \\[\\begin{pmatrix}1-c^2&-c\\sqrt{1-c^2}\\\\-c\\sqrt{1-c^2}&-(1-c^2)\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Its trace is zero and its determinant is \\(-(1-c^2)\\), so its eigenvalues are \\(\\pm\\sqrt{1-c^2}\\). All remaining eigenvalues are zero. Taking half the sum of absolute eigenvalues gives \\(T=\\sqrt{1-c^2}\\). Since \\(F=c^2\\), we obtain \\(T=\\sqrt{1-F}\\). The identical-state case c=1 gives T=0 directly. For mixed states, equality need not hold. The general bounds are \\[1-\\sqrt{F(\\rho,\\sigma)}\\le T(\\rho,\\sigma)\\le\\sqrt{1-F(\\rho,\\sigma)}.\\] These are the Fuchs–van de Graaf inequalities, stated here without their full proof; see Watrous, Chapter 3 for the fidelity and distance results. They let us translate a fidelity guarantee into a bound on measurement distinguishability. For example, F≥0.99 implies T≤0.1. It does not imply T≤0.01. The square root matters when reporting an error budget. Consider \\(\\rho=|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\) and \\(\\sigma=I/2\\). Here F=1/2, but subtracting the matrices gives eigenvalues ±1/2, hence T=1/2. This is smaller than \\(\\sqrt{1-F}=1/\\sqrt2\\). Our pure-state identity cannot be carried over just because one of the two states is pure. Fidelity also obeys a processing theorem: \\(F(\\mathcal N(\\rho),\\mathcal N(\\sigma))\\ge F(\\rho,\\sigma)\\). We state this result rather than infer it from the trace-distance inequalities, which do not by themselves prove it. The physical interpretation agrees with our earlier argument: noise can make states look alike by erasing the distinctions between them. Worked check 1.2. Compare \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle=(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) before and after complete dephasing, which sets off-diagonal entries to zero in the computational basis. Reasoned solution The input vectors have zero overlap, so F=0 and T=1. Their density matrices are \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\-1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). Dephasing sends both to I/2, giving F=1 and T=0. Before noise, an X-basis measurement distinguishes them perfectly. Afterwards no measurement can distinguish them. Their identical initial diagonal entries never meant that their full input states were identical."}, {"title": "Asymmetric tests", "chapter": "Distinguishability and Testing", "url": "01-distinguishability.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Asymmetric tests 1.3.1 Constraints instead of equal costs So far, we have maximized the probability of giving the correct label. Both kinds of wrong answer enter the average error with their prior weights. But imagine that \\(\\rho\\) describes a signal that a detector must rarely miss, while \\(\\sigma\\) describes background noise. A false alarm and a missed signal may have quite different consequences. We should formulate the task to reflect that distinction. Continue to let M mean “accept the signal hypothesis \\(\\rho\\).” There are now two separate error probabilities: \\[\\alpha=1-\\tr(M\\rho)\\quad\\text{(missed signal)},\\qquad \\beta=\\tr(M\\sigma)\\quad\\text{(false alarm)}.\\] Suppose the missed-signal probability must be at most \\(\\epsilon\\). Among all tests meeting that requirement, we want the one with the smallest false-alarm probability. The problem is \\[\\beta_\\epsilon=\\min_M\\tr(M\\sigma)\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad 0\\le M\\le I,\\quad\\tr(M\\rho)\\ge1-\\epsilon.\\] Notice what changed. We no longer need a prior to state this task: each error is conditional on one of the two hypotheses, and the acceptable miss rate is supplied externally. The earlier minimum-average-error measurement need not be optimal for this constrained problem. A mathematical expression is useful only after we have checked that it answers the operational question being asked. 1.3.2 Spending a false-alarm budget Let us solve a small case completely. Take commuting states whose diagonal probabilities are \\(\\rho=(0.8,0.2)\\) and \\(\\sigma=(0.3,0.7)\\). A measurement in their shared eigenbasis reveals an outcome, call it a or b. No off-diagonal entry of M contributes to either trace, so we can replace M by its diagonal without changing either error. Write those diagonal entries as \\(m_a,m_b\\), each between zero and one: the probabilities of accepting the signal after seeing a or b. We then have \\(\\alpha=1-0.8m_a-0.2m_b\\) and \\(\\beta=0.3m_a+0.7m_b\\). If \\(\\epsilon=0.2\\), choosing \\(m_a=1,m_b=0\\) accepts 80% of true signals and falsely accepts 30% of background trials. Could a mixture of the two outcomes do better? Compare what each acceptance buys us. Increasing \\(m_a\\) by a small amount raises signal acceptance by 0.8 times that amount and raises false alarms by 0.3 times it. The false-alarm cost per unit of signal acceptance is therefore 0.3/0.8=0.375. For b it is 0.7/0.2=3.5. Outcome a supplies signal acceptance much more cheaply. An optimum must use a as much as possible before paying for b; otherwise we could shift acceptance from b to a, preserve signal acceptance, and reduce false alarms. This is the likelihood-ratio principle in a two-outcome example. The ratios \\(\\rho_i/\\sigma_i\\) tell us which observations favor the signal most strongly. We have derived the ordering by comparing costs, rather than merely asserting a decision rule. Worked check 1.3. Now tighten the requirement to \\(\\alpha\\le0.1\\). Find an optimal test and its false-alarm probability. Reasoned solution We need signal acceptance at least 0.9. Use a fully, giving 0.8, then obtain the remaining 0.1 from b. Thus \\(0.8+0.2m_b=0.9\\), so \\(m_b=0.5\\). The effect is \\(M=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.5)\\), and \\(\\beta=0.3+0.7(0.5)=0.65\\). We accept every a outcome and toss a fair coin after b. The cost comparison proves optimality: replacing any acceptance of a by acceptance of b would increase the false-alarm rate. Tightening the miss requirement from 0.2 to 0.1 has forced a substantial increase in false alarms. 1.3.3 From a solved example to a general method Our optimal effect \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.5)\\) is not a projector. This illustrates why we allowed general effects from the beginning. A valid test can include deliberate randomness. For the minimum-average-error problem, the spectral argument showed that a projector always suffices. A constrained test has a different optimum, so we must not impose that earlier conclusion without checking it. For states that do not commute, there may be no shared eigenbasis that turns the problem into classical outcome probabilities. Nevertheless, the matrix optimization remains well defined. It is a semidefinite program : the quantity to minimize and the scalar constraint are linear in the entries of a Hermitian matrix, while positivity restricts the allowed matrices. These features give us a systematic route beyond hand-solvable examples. Why is convexity useful here? If \\(M_1\\) and \\(M_2\\) both meet the miss constraint, then \\(tM_1+(1-t)M_2\\), with \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), also meets it. Physically, we can flip a coin to choose between the two tests. Their errors average with the same weights. But feasibility is not optimality. A numerical measurement with false-alarm probability b shows that \\(\\beta_\\epsilon\\le b\\). To certify the optimum, we also need a matching lower bound applying to all feasible measurements, just as the spectral proof supplied a bound for our first task. We have moved from a concrete guessing experiment to two general ways of assessing a test: its average success, or one error minimized under a constraint on the other. Both concern one supplied system. If we are given many independently prepared copies, we can ask how rapidly the error decreases with their number; Chapter 3 will connect that question to relative entropy. First, Chapter 2 will examine the physical processes that carry, alter, or discard the distinctions we have learned to measure."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-1", "text": "State the trace-preserving condition on Kraus operators. \\(\\sum_kK_k^\\dagger K_k=I\\). The opposite ordering describes unitality, which is different."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-2", "text": "What is the Choi trace for a qutrit-input channel in our convention? It is 3, regardless of the output dimension."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-3", "text": "Why is a negative Choi eigenvalue a failure certificate? The normalized Choi matrix is the output on half of a maximally entangled state. A negative eigenvalue makes that output nonphysical."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute amplitude damping on |1⟩ at γ=0.2. The output is diag(0.2,0.8). The environment records a jump with probability 0.2."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-5", "text": "Is amplitude damping unital at nonzero γ? No. The output is diag((1+γ)/2,(1−γ)/2), unequal to I/2 for γ>0."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-6", "text": "Find an isometry for the dephasing map \\((1-p)\\rho+pZ\\rho Z\\). \\(V|\\psi\\rangle=\\sqrt{1-p}|\\psi\\rangle|0\\rangle+\\sqrt p Z|\\psi\\rangle|1\\rangle\\). Orthogonal labels give \\(V^\\dagger V=I\\), and tracing them out gives the desired map."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-7", "text": "Bound a four-gate circuit’s diamond error if each replacement has distance at most 0.003. The distance is at most 0.012, hence the output trace distance is at most 0.006 for any input and reference."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does a positive map always have a positive Choi matrix? No. Complete positivity, not mere positivity, is equivalent to Choi positivity."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does measuring computational-basis inputs certify an identity channel? No. Dephasing agrees on every computational-basis state while erasing off-diagonal coherence."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels and Dilations — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#exercise-10", "text": "Show that mixing two channels is again a channel. For weight t, use √t times the first list and √(1−t) times the second. Completeness follows from tI+(1−t)I=I; the resulting map is the desired mixture."}, {"title": "Complete positivity", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Complete positivity 2.1.1 An entangled input is a stronger test A channel \\(\\mathcal N:A\\to B\\) is linear, completely positive, and trace preserving. Linearity respects preparation mixtures. Trace preservation keeps total probability one. Complete positivity requires \\(\\operatorname{id}_R\\otimes\\mathcal N\\) to preserve positivity for every reference system, because a device must work when its input is part of a larger experiment. The reference need not be available to the device. Ordinary transposition is a useful counterexample. It preserves the eigenvalues and trace of an isolated density matrix. Apply it to the second half of \\(|\\Phi\\rangle=(|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\), however. The resulting operator is one half of the swap operator. Its antisymmetric eigenvector \\((|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) has eigenvalue \\(-1/2\\). This is not a state. Positivity alone therefore fails the physical consistency test. We fix the Choi convention explicitly. Let \\(|\\Omega\\rangle=\\sum_i|i\\rangle_R|i\\rangle_A\\), without normalization, and define \\(J_{\\mathcal N}=(\\operatorname{id}_R\\otimes\\mathcal N)(|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|)\\). Complete positivity is equivalent to \\(J_{\\mathcal N}\\ge0\\); trace preservation is equivalent to \\(\\tr_BJ_{\\mathcal N}=I_R\\). Thus \\(\\tr J_{\\mathcal N}=d_A\\), not one. The normalized Choi state is \\(J/d_A\\). The reconstruction formula is \\(\\mathcal N(X)=\\tr_R[(X^T\\otimes I)J]\\), in the basis used for \\(|\\Omega\\rangle\\). Worked check 2.1. What is the trace of the Choi matrix of the identity qubit channel? Reasoned solution It is 2: \\(J=|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|\\) and \\(\\langle\\Omega|\\Omega\\rangle=2\\). Dividing by 2 gives a Bell-state density matrix."}, {"title": "Kraus operators and environments", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 Kraus operators and environments 2.2.1 Where the missing coherence goes A Kraus representation writes \\(\\mathcal N(X)=\\sum_kK_kXK_k^\\dagger\\), with \\(\\sum_kK_k^\\dagger K_k=I\\). It is completely positive because each term stays positive even after adjoining a reference. Trace preservation follows by moving factors around the trace. Conversely, a positive Choi matrix can be spectrally decomposed and its eigenvectors reshaped into Kraus matrices; this proves the finite-dimensional representation theorem. For amplitude damping, use \\(K_0=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\sqrt{1-\\gamma})\\) and \\(K_1=\\sqrt\\gamma|0\\rangle\\langle1|\\), where \\(0\\le\\gamma\\le1\\). An input with entries \\(\\rho_{00}=a,\\rho_{01}=b,\\rho_{11}=1-a\\) becomes \\[\\mathcal N(\\rho)=\\begin{pmatrix}a+\\gamma(1-a)&\\sqrt{1-\\gamma}\\,b\\\\\\sqrt{1-\\gamma}\\,b^*&(1-\\gamma)(1-a)\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Define an isometry \\(V|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_kK_k|\\psi\\rangle|k\\rangle_E\\). Completeness gives \\(V^\\dagger V=I\\); discarding \\(E\\) yields the channel. Discarding \\(B\\) instead defines a complementary channel, which records what the environment learns. Kraus representations are not unique, so their labels are not automatically physical detector outcomes. An actual measurement of the environment supplies an instrument and a classical record. The equivalence of these representations is treated in Watrous, Chapter 2 . We use the isometry later to compare information reaching the receiver and environment. Worked check 2.2. Apply damping with γ=0.36 to |+⟩. Reasoned solution The output is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}0.68&0.4\\\\0.4&0.32\\end{pmatrix}\\). Its trace is one and determinant is 0.0576, so it is positive. Damping changes populations as well as coherence."}, {"title": "Comparing devices", "chapter": "Quantum Channels and Dilations", "url": "02-quantum-channels.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 Comparing devices 2.3.1 A reference can expose hidden errors Comparing channels on a few chosen states does not certify their behavior on every input. A stronger distance allows a reference: \\(\\|\\mathcal N-\\mathcal M\\|_\\diamond=\\max_{\\rho_{RA}}\\|(\\operatorname{id}_R\\otimes(\\mathcal N-\\mathcal M))(\\rho_{RA})\\|_1\\), where for channel differences a reference of input dimension suffices. Equal-prior single-use discrimination succeeds with probability \\(1/2+\\|\\mathcal N-\\mathcal M\\|_\\diamond/4\\). This characterization is a theorem, not a conclusion from a small test set. Consider identity versus complete dephasing on a qubit. Both act identically on \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\); basis-state testing misses the error entirely. On \\(|+\\rangle\\), the outputs are \\(|+\\rangle\\langle+|\\) and \\(I/2\\), whose trace distance is one half. This already permits success 0.75. It proves a lower bound on the optimized device-discrimination success. To establish optimality one still needs an upper bound. For a sequence of devices, the triangle inequality and contraction by channels imply a useful telescoping bound: replacing \\(m\\) devices whose diamond distances are at most \\(\\delta_i\\) changes the overall channel by at most \\(\\sum_i\\delta_i\\). This is conservative but applies to entangled and adaptive inputs when the surrounding operations are channels. State-specific accuracy cannot simply be substituted into this worst-case statement. Worked check 2.3. What is the diamond distance between identity and the Pauli-X channel? Reasoned solution Input |0⟩ produces the orthogonal outputs |0⟩ and |1⟩, with trace norm difference 2. No pair of channels has diamond distance greater than 2, so the distance is exactly 2 and one use distinguishes them perfectly."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-1", "text": "What is the entropy of a pure qutrit? It is zero bits. Measurement outcomes can still be random in another basis."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-2", "text": "What is the maximum entropy of two qubits? The dimension is four, so the maximum is log₂4=2 bits, attained by I/4."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-3", "text": "Compute mutual information for a product state. S(AB)=S(A)+S(B), hence I(A:B)=0."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute D(|0⟩⟨0| ∥ I/2) and the reverse. The first is one bit. The reverse is infinite because I/2 has support outside the one-dimensional support of |0⟩."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-5", "text": "Compute S(A|B) for a product of two maximally mixed qubits. S(A|B)=2−1=1 bit. Without correlation, conditioning on B changes nothing."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-6", "text": "Show the mutual-information chain rule. \\(I(A:BC)-I(A:B)=S(BC)-S(ABC)-S(B)+S(AB)=I(A:C|B)\\)."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-7", "text": "For a pure tripartite state, simplify I(A:C|B). \\(S(AB)=S(C), S(BC)=S(A), S(B)=S(AC)\\), so \\(I(A:C|B)=I(A:C)\\)."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-8", "text": "Is mutual information an entanglement measure? No. A separable mixture of |00⟩ and |11⟩ has mutual information one bit. Mutual information counts total correlations."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does data processing say entropy always increases? No. Relative entropy contracts when both arguments undergo the same channel. An individual state’s entropy may increase or decrease."}, {"title": "Entropy and Data Processing — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#exercise-10", "text": "Explain why relative entropy is additive on independent copies. \\(\\log(\\rho\\otimes\\rho)=\\log\\rho\\otimes I+I\\otimes\\log\\rho\\) on the support. Trace factorization gives \\(D(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\|\\sigma^{\\otimes n})=nD(\\rho\\|\\sigma)\\)."}, {"title": "Entropy and relative entropy", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Entropy and relative entropy 3.1.1 Spectra, support, and the reference state The entropy of a state is \\(S(\\rho)=-\\tr\\rho\\log_2\\rho=-\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\log_2\\lambda_i\\), with \\(0\\log0=0\\). It measures the spread of the spectrum, not the spread of outcomes in an arbitrary basis. A pure qubit has zero entropy even if an incompatible measurement gives a uniformly random bit. The maximal entropy in dimension \\(d\\) is \\(\\log_2d\\), reached by \\(I/d\\). Relative entropy compares a state with a reference: \\(D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)=\\tr\\rho(\\log_2\\rho-\\log_2\\sigma)\\) when \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\rho\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}\\sigma\\), and infinity otherwise. It is nonnegative but generally asymmetric and does not obey a triangle inequality. For diagonal states it becomes the classical expression \\(\\sum_i p_i\\log_2(p_i/q_i)\\). The support condition is operational: assigning zero probability to an event that actually occurs is a decisive modeling failure. To evaluate a noncommuting example, diagonalize each matrix separately. The term \\(\\tr\\rho\\log\\sigma\\) depends on how the eigenvectors overlap; subtracting two lists of eigenvalues is not enough. For the special reference \\(I/d\\), however, \\(\\log_2(I/d)=-(\\log_2d)I\\), giving \\(D(\\rho\\|I/d)=\\log_2d-S(\\rho)\\). This identity measures deviation from maximal mixing and will later provide a resource monotone. Worked check 3.1. Compute \\(D(\\operatorname{diag}(0.8,0.2)\\|I/2)\\). Reasoned solution The binary entropy is \\(h_2(0.2)\\approx0.721928\\), so the relative entropy is \\(1-h_2(0.2)\\approx0.278072\\) bits."}, {"title": "Correlations and conditioning", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Correlations and conditioning 3.2.1 Negative conditional entropy is consistent For a bipartite state, define \\(I(A:B)=S(A)+S(B)-S(AB)\\) and \\(S(A|B)=S(AB)-S(B)\\). Expanding the logarithm of \\(\\rho_A\\otimes\\rho_B\\) shows \\(I(A:B)=D(\\rho_{AB}\\|\\rho_A\\otimes\\rho_B)\\). Thus mutual information is nonnegative and vanishes precisely for product states. It counts total correlation, including classical correlation; it is not an entanglement measure. A Bell pair has entropies \\(S(A)=S(B)=1\\), \\(S(AB)=0\\), giving \\(I(A:B)=2\\) and \\(S(A|B)=-1\\). Dephasing one half yields \\((|00\\rangle\\langle00|+|11\\rangle\\langle11|)/2\\), with joint entropy one. Its mutual information is one and conditional entropy zero. The local states have not changed, but coherent correlations have been lost. The central theorem is data processing: for a channel \\(\\mathcal N\\), \\(D(\\mathcal N(\\rho)\\|\\mathcal N(\\sigma))\\le D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)\\). We state the full operator inequality without proof; its noncommuting proof is substantially deeper than the diagonal case. Applying it to a channel on one half proves that local processing cannot increase mutual information. Applying it to \\(\\rho_{ABC}\\) and \\(\\rho_A\\otimes\\rho_{BC}\\), then tracing out \\(C\\), gives strong subadditivity: \\[I(A:C|B)=S(AB)+S(BC)-S(B)-S(ABC)\\ge0.\\] See Watrous, Chapter 5 for entropy inequalities and their proofs. The derivation of strong subadditivity here depends on the stated data-processing theorem, rather than proving that theorem. Worked check 3.2. For three classical copies of one fair bit, compute \\(I(A:C|B)\\). Reasoned solution Every nonempty subset has entropy one, so \\(I(A:C|B)=1+1-1-1=0\\). A and C are correlated without conditioning, but B already supplies the common bit."}, {"title": "Many-copy testing", "chapter": "Entropy and Data Processing", "url": "03-entropy-and-data-processing.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Many-copy testing 3.3.1 Relative entropy as an error exponent Return to the asymmetric test in Chapter 1, now with hypotheses \\(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\) and \\(\\sigma^{\\otimes n}\\). Fix a tolerated miss probability \\(0<\\epsilon<1\\), independently of \\(n\\), and minimize the false-acceptance probability \\(\\beta_{n,\\epsilon}\\). Quantum Stein’s lemma states that \\(-\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}n^{-1}\\log_2\\beta_{n,\\epsilon}=D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)\\) for finite-dimensional states with finite relative entropy. Collective measurements are allowed. The Ogawa–Nagaoka strong-converse result completes the quantum Stein theorem. This is a theorem statement, not a proof; the asymptotic testing discussion in Preskill’s notes provides further context. Why should a logarithm appear? For commuting states, probabilities of independent strings multiply. Their likelihood ratio is a product, and its logarithm is a sum. Under the first hypothesis, the average log likelihood approaches \\(\\sum_i p_i\\log_2(p_i/q_i)\\). This gives an intuition for the exponent; controlling error tails and noncommuting measurements needs additional arguments. For the biased coin and fair coin above, the exponent is approximately 0.278072 bits per sample. That does not assert the exact finite-sample formula \\(\\beta=2^{-0.278072n}\\); subleading terms and the allowed miss probability matter. Nor does it describe equal-prior minimum-error testing, where both errors are penalized symmetrically. Keeping these tasks separate prevents an entropy formula from being applied to the wrong experiment. Worked check 3.3. Does the exponent alone determine the false-positive probability at n=20? Reasoned solution No. It specifies the limiting normalized logarithm. Finite-block bounds or an explicit test are needed at n=20; substituting the leading exponential is only an asymptotic estimate."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-1", "text": "Find the Schmidt probabilities of √0.9|00⟩+√0.1|11⟩. They are (0.9,0.1); their sum is one."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-2", "text": "Can (0.6,0.4) convert deterministically to (0.9,0.1)? Yes. 0.6≤0.9 and totals agree. The initial state is more entangled."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-3", "text": "Can a product state convert deterministically to a Bell pair by LOCC? No: 1≤0.5 fails. The allowed operations cannot create entanglement from a product input."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-4", "text": "Find the success probability of the same equalizing filter on probabilities (0.9,0.1). Use \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(1/3,1)\\). Each surviving squared amplitude is 0.1, so success probability is 0.2."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-5", "text": "Complete the filter diag(1/2,1) into a two-outcome instrument. The failure operator can be \\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt3/2,0)\\). Its squared product fills the missing diagonal (3/4,0)."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compute pure-state entanglement for equal Schmidt probabilities on four levels. The entropy is log₂4=2 bits, corresponding asymptotically to two Bell pairs per copy."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-7", "text": "Prove that partial transpose preserves positivity for a separable state. If \\(\\rho=\\sum_jp_j\\alpha_j\\otimes\\beta_j\\), then \\(\\rho^{T_B}=\\sum_jp_j\\alpha_j\\otimes\\beta_j^T\\). Each term is positive and the weights are nonnegative."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does equal reduced-state entropy imply two mixed states have equal entanglement? No. Both have maximally mixed marginals, while the shared-bit mixture is separable and the Bell pair is entangled."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does a concentration rate of 0.72 mean one copy yields exactly 0.72 Bell pairs? No. It is the limiting ratio of output Bell pairs to input copies for large block protocols with vanishing error."}, {"title": "Entanglement as a Resource — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#exercise-10", "text": "Give two incomparable Schmidt vectors and check both prefixes. For (0.5,0.25,0.25) and (0.45,0.4,0.15), the prefixes are 0.5>0.45 and 0.75<0.85. Neither vector majorizes the other."}, {"title": "The allowed operations", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 The allowed operations 4.1.1 Local control with classical messages Alice and Bob share a state but cannot send quantum systems to one another. They may append local ancillas, perform local instruments, exchange classical messages, and choose later operations based on the transcript. These are local operations and classical communication, abbreviated LOCC. Shared randomness is allowed; shared entanglement is a resource to be counted. A joint entangling gate between their laboratories is not free. A bipartite pure state has a Schmidt decomposition \\(|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_i\\sqrt{\\lambda_i}|i\\rangle_A|i\\rangle_B\\), where the nonnegative probabilities sum to one. Local basis changes do not change the \\(\\lambda_i\\). The reduced-state entropy \\(H(\\lambda)\\) quantifies pure-state entanglement in the asymptotic setting, but the full vector controls exact single-copy conversions. For \\(\\sqrt{0.8}|00\\rangle+\\sqrt{0.2}|11\\rangle\\), the local entropy is approximately 0.721928 bits. Compare the separable mixture \\(0.8|00\\rangle\\langle00|+0.2|11\\rangle\\langle11|\\): its local entropy is the same, yet its entanglement is zero because Alice can sample a classical label and tell Bob which local state to prepare. The global coherence distinguishes the resources. Reduced-state entropy alone therefore fails as an entanglement measure for general mixed states. Worked check 4.1. Why can LOCC prepare the correlated mixture above but not a Bell pair from a product input? Reasoned solution The mixture is obtained by shared classical randomness and local preparations. Each conditioned LOCC branch maps a product input to a product state, and forgetting branches gives a separable mixture. A Bell pair is not separable."}, {"title": "Majorization", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Majorization 4.2.1 The direction of deterministic conversion Sort the Schmidt probabilities in descending order. Write \\(\\lambda\\prec\\mu\\) if \\(\\sum_{i=1}^k\\lambda_i\\le\\sum_{i=1}^k\\mu_i\\) for every proper prefix, with equal totals. Pad shorter vectors with zeros. Nielsen’s theorem states that a bipartite pure state with vector \\(\\lambda\\) converts exactly and deterministically by LOCC to one with vector \\(\\mu\\) if and only if \\(\\lambda\\prec\\mu\\). We use the theorem; its full constructive proof is outside this chapter. The direction is easy to reverse accidentally. A Bell vector \\((0.5,0.5)\\) is majorized by \\((0.8,0.2)\\), so the Bell pair can be degraded into that target. The reverse deterministic transformation is impossible. Entropy decreases, consistent with the operational restriction, but entropy alone is not sufficient to compare higher-dimensional vectors. The two-dimensional conversion can be shown explicitly. On her half of a Bell pair, Alice measures \\(A_0=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{0.8},\\sqrt{0.2})\\) and \\(A_1=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{0.2},\\sqrt{0.8})\\). Their squared products sum to identity. Each outcome has probability one half. The first produces the target; the second produces its swapped version. Alice sends the outcome, and both parties apply X for the second outcome. Every branch then has the desired state. Worked check 4.2. Are vectors (0.5,0.25,0.25) and (0.45,0.4,0.15) comparable by majorization? Reasoned solution No. The first prefix is 0.5 versus 0.45, but the second is 0.75 versus 0.85. The inequalities change direction, so neither exact deterministic LOCC conversion is possible."}, {"title": "Many copies and mixed states", "chapter": "Entanglement as a Resource", "url": "04-entanglement-transformations.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 Many copies and mixed states 4.3.1 What an entanglement rate promises A probabilistic filter can concentrate one pair. For the state with probabilities \\((0.8,0.2)\\), Alice’s successful Kraus operator \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,1)\\) makes the two surviving amplitudes equal. The unnormalized state has amplitudes \\(\\sqrt{0.2}\\) on both terms, so success probability is 0.4 and the normalized result is a Bell pair. A second Kraus operator completes the instrument. Failure must be reported; ignoring it would falsely turn a conditional success into a deterministic protocol. With many independent copies of a fixed pure state, concentration and dilution become asymptotically reversible: the Bell-pair rate is \\(H(\\lambda)\\), with vanishing error and unrestricted classical communication. This is a coding theorem, stated rather than proved here. The typical-subspace reasoning in the next chapter explains why entropy appears. It does not imply exact conversion of one copy into a fractional Bell pair. Mixed states are harder. Distillable entanglement measures the asymptotic Bell-pair yield, while entanglement cost measures the Bell pairs needed to prepare the state. These need not agree. A useful entanglement witness is the partial transpose: every separable state has positive partial transpose, since transposition preserves each local factor’s positivity. A negative eigenvalue certifies entanglement. Positive partial transpose alone does not certify separability in general dimensions. This chapter establishes the test’s necessity directly, without asserting a general converse. Worked check 4.3. Find a negative partial-transpose eigenvalue for a Bell state. Reasoned solution The partial transpose is swap/2. On the antisymmetric vector it has eigenvalue −1/2, proving entanglement. This repeats the same matrix calculation that ruled out transposition as a physical channel."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-1", "text": "Compute the entropy of a source with eigenvalues (1/2,1/2). The entropy is one bit, so faithful blind compression has threshold one qubit per signal."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-2", "text": "What is the dimension of the full space for n qubits? It is 2ⁿ, corresponding to n qubits of unrestricted memory."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-3", "text": "If typical mass is 0.99, what fidelity lower bound follows? The bound is 0.9801; this is a lower bound, not necessarily the exact achieved fidelity."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-4", "text": "Count binary strings of length 6 with at most one 1. There are 1+6=7 strings. Their span fits in three qubits."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-5", "text": "For that length-6 subspace and p=0.2, compute retained probability. The mass is 0.8⁶+6(0.2)(0.8⁵)=0.65536. The squared-mass fidelity bound is about 0.429497."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-6", "text": "Derive the upper bound on typical-subspace dimension. If there are K typical strings, \\(1\\ge K2^{-n(S+\\delta)}\\), so \\(K\\le2^{n(S+\\delta)}\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-7", "text": "Show why keeping just the most probable individual string fails for p=0.2. The all-zero string has probability 0.8ⁿ, tending to zero. High-probability storage requires many strings, not one representative."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does preserving the average state imply preserving the source’s quantum information? No. A replacement channel preserves that average state but destroys correlations with a purifying reference."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-9", "text": "Can the i.i.d. formula be applied unchanged to an arbitrary correlated source? No. Independence supplied products of eigenvalues and a law-of-large-numbers argument. Correlated sources require an appropriate entropy-rate theorem and assumptions."}, {"title": "Quantum Compression — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design a finite-block study of a biased binary source. For each n, choose a Hamming-weight cutoff, sum its binomial dimension and probability, and report ceiling(log₂ dimension) and squared retained mass. Plot memory rate against the certified fidelity bound; do not label the bound an exact optimum."}, {"title": "Typical sequences", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Typical sequences 5.1.1 Probability concentrates, not individual outcomes Let a source independently emit systems with average state \\(\\rho\\). In its eigenbasis, \\(\\rho=\\sum_i\\lambda_i|i\\rangle\\langle i|\\), and \\(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\) has eigenvalues \\(\\lambda_{i_1}\\cdots\\lambda_{i_n}\\). Define a string as \\(\\delta\\)-typical if its negative log probability per symbol differs from \\(S(\\rho)\\) by at most \\(\\delta\\). The law of large numbers says the total probability of these strings tends to one. It does not say a particular typical string becomes likely. Each typical string has probability between \\(2^{-n(S+\\delta)}\\) and \\(2^{-n(S-\\delta)}\\). Therefore the number of typical strings is at most \\(2^{n(S+\\delta)}\\). If their total mass is at least \\(1-\\epsilon\\), their number is at least \\((1-\\epsilon)2^{n(S-\\delta)}\\). These bounds are the bridge between entropy and storage dimension. The upper bound comes from the fact that probabilities sum to at most one; the lower bound uses the largest permitted probability per typical string. For spectrum \\((0.8,0.2)\\), typical strings contain about \\(0.2n\\) occurrences of the second eigenvector. Their count grows at exponential rate \\(h_2(0.2)\\approx0.721928\\). The entire Hilbert space has dimension \\(2^n\\), but almost all source weight occupies a much smaller spectral subspace. “Almost all” refers to this source distribution, not to all possible input states. Worked check 5.1. What storage rate does the typical-subspace dimension suggest for this source? Reasoned solution Approximately 0.721928 qubits per signal asymptotically. For a rigorous construction choose a rate strictly above that entropy, then let the block size grow; the finite-block overhead cannot be dropped without analysis."}, {"title": "Coherent encoding", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Coherent encoding 5.2.1 Preserve the reference, not only the density matrix Let \\(\\Pi\\) project onto the typical subspace, whose dimension fits in a memory of \\(m\\) qubits. An encoder maps that subspace isometrically into the memory. A decoder reverses the isometry. The atypical component can be mapped to a fixed fallback state to complete a trace-preserving channel. No measurement distinguishing the individual typical eigenvectors is needed. Their superpositions must remain coherent. The appropriate error test introduces a purification \\(|\\psi\\rangle_{RA}\\) of the source state. Entanglement fidelity is \\(F_e=\\langle\\psi|(\\operatorname{id}_R\\otimes\\mathcal D\\mathcal E)(|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|)|\\psi\\rangle\\). It tests whether encoding and decoding preserve correlations with a reference. Simply checking \\(\\mathcal D\\mathcal E(\\rho)\\approx\\rho\\) is insufficient: a device could throw away the input and prepare \\(\\rho\\) anew, passing that weak test while losing every reference correlation. For a compression channel with one Kraus operator equal to \\(\\Pi\\) and all other branches acting on its complement, the identity \\(F_e=\\sum_k|\\tr(\\rho A_k)|^2\\) gives \\(F_e\\ge[\\tr(\\rho\\Pi)]^2\\). If typical weight is at least \\(1-\\epsilon\\), then fidelity is at least \\((1-\\epsilon)^2\\). This direct estimate explains why high typical weight supports faithful quantum storage without inspecting the encoded information. Worked check 5.2. Why does complete dephasing fail to compress a maximally mixed qubit faithfully even though it preserves I/2? Reasoned solution Purify I/2 with a Bell pair. Dephasing one half produces the equal mixture of |00⟩ and |11⟩, whose overlap with the Bell pair is 1/2. The reduced state passes, but entanglement fidelity does not."}, {"title": "The coding theorem and finite blocks", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "05-quantum-compression.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 The coding theorem and finite blocks 5.3.1 A rate statement needs a source model Schumacher compression, stated here in its memoryless finite-dimensional form, says that a source with average state \\(\\rho\\) can be compressed at any rate above \\(S(\\rho)\\) qubits per signal with entanglement fidelity tending to one. Rates below the entropy cannot attain asymptotically faithful storage in this blind encoding model. The typical-subspace construction gives the achievability mechanism; the full converse is a theorem whose proof we do not reproduce. See Watrous, Chapter 5 for a precise source-coding treatment. A small block makes the overhead tangible. With ten independent signals and second-eigenvector probability 0.2, keep strings containing at most three such symbols. Their subspace has dimension \\(1+10+45+120=176\\), requiring eight qubits. Its probability is \\(\\sum_{k=0}^3\\binom{10}{k}0.2^k0.8^{10-k}\\approx0.879126\\). The simple fidelity lower bound is about 0.772863. An asymptotic rate near 0.722 therefore does not promise near-perfect storage in eight qubits for this block. Correlated sources, unknown source states, and worst-case storage are different problems. If every state in a \\(2^n\\)-dimensional space must be preserved, no source bias is available to exploit. The next chapters change the resource from memory dimension to uses of a noisy transmission channel, where both encoding and decoding must combat environmental loss. Worked check 5.3. If a source always emits a known pure state, how many asymptotic storage qubits are needed? Reasoned solution Zero per signal. The decoder can prepare the known state. Its source purification has no nontrivial reference correlation, unlike an unknown arbitrary pure input that must be preserved."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-1", "text": "Compute χ when every signal state is identical. χ=0. No label information reaches the receiver through the signal."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-2", "text": "What units does classical channel capacity use? Bits per channel use, with the error criterion and assistance model specified."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-3", "text": "What is the maximum Holevo information of a qubit ensemble? At most one bit without an additional receiver system; orthogonal pure signals attain it."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-4", "text": "Evaluate χ for two equally likely pure signals with overlap magnitude 0.6. χ=h₂(0.2)≈0.721928 bits."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-5", "text": "Compute the qubit erasure classical capacity at p=0.6. C=0.4 bits per use."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-6", "text": "Derive the block-diagonal entropy identity. Expand \\(-\\sum_{x,i}p_x\\lambda_{xi}\\log_2(p_x\\lambda_{xi})\\). The two terms are \\(H(p)\\) and \\(\\sum_xp_xS(\\rho_x)\\)."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-7", "text": "Does an ensemble with χ=0.7 establish C≤0.7? No. It supplies a rate achievable asymptotically with appropriate coding, hence a lower bound on capacity. An upper bound must cover every allowed code."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does the Holevo bound prohibit superdense coding? No. Superdense coding includes preshared entanglement; the receiver measures the transmitted system together with their existing half. The unassisted qubit-ensemble model does not describe that resource."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does a collective measurement require entangled transmitted codewords? No. A decoder can jointly measure a tensor product of individually prepared signals. Entangled encoding is a distinct option."}, {"title": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#exercise-10", "text": "Derive χ for a general ensemble through the erasure channel. The common flag term \\(h_2(p)\\) cancels. The result is \\(\\chi_{\\rm out}=(1-p)\\chi_{\\rm in}\\), because the flag probability is independent of the label."}, {"title": "An ensemble is a communication model", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 An ensemble is a communication model 6.1.1 The Holevo quantity A sender samples a classical label \\(X=x\\) with probability \\(p_x\\), prepares \\(\\rho_x\\), and transmits it. The joint description is \\(\\rho_{XB}=\\sum_xp_x|x\\rangle\\langle x|\\otimes\\rho_x\\). Because the label blocks are orthogonal, its entropy is \\(H(p)+\\sum_xp_xS(\\rho_x)\\). Consequently, \\(I(X:B)=S(\\bar\\rho)-\\sum_xp_xS(\\rho_x)\\), where \\(\\bar\\rho=\\sum_xp_x\\rho_x\\). This expression is the Holevo quantity \\(\\chi\\). The receiver measures B and obtains a classical record Y. Measurement is a channel, so data processing gives \\(I(X:Y)\\le I(X:B)=\\chi\\). This is the Holevo bound. Maximizing the left-hand side over all POVMs defines accessible information. It need not equal \\(\\chi\\). The bound separates information present in a quantum ensemble from information accessible through a chosen single-copy measurement. For two equally likely pure states with overlap magnitude \\(c\\), the average state has eigenvalues \\((1+c)/2\\) and \\((1-c)/2\\). Each signal has zero entropy, so \\(\\chi=h_2((1-c)/2)\\). For |0⟩ and |+⟩ this is approximately 0.600876 bits. Continuously many possible amplitudes have not created continuously many perfectly distinguishable messages. Worked check 6.1. What is χ for equally likely orthogonal qubit signals? Reasoned solution The average state is I/2 and the individual signals are pure, so χ=1−0=1 bit. Measuring in their common orthogonal basis attains the bound."}, {"title": "Coding over independent uses", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Coding over independent uses 6.2.1 Why a capacity may need regularization A block code encodes one of \\(M_n\\) messages into \\(n\\) uses of a memoryless channel \\(\\mathcal N\\), followed by a joint decoding measurement. Its rate is \\(\\log_2M_n/n\\). We define unassisted classical capacity using uniformly distributed messages and average decoding error tending to zero, with no preshared entanglement or free side communication. Arbitrary input states across a block are allowed. For one use, define \\(\\chi^*(\\mathcal N)=\\max_{\\{p_x,\\rho_x\\}}\\chi(\\{p_x,\\mathcal N(\\rho_x)\\})\\). Product-state coding and collective decoding attain rates approaching this quantity. With arbitrary entangled block inputs the full expression is \\(C(\\mathcal N)=\\sup_{n\\ge1}\\chi^*(\\mathcal N^{\\otimes n})/n\\). This coding theorem is stated with its resource assumptions; it is not proved by applying the single-copy Holevo bound. See Watrous, Chapter 8 . Regularization matters: optimizing one-use ensembles does not generally settle the unrestricted capacity. Conversely, a particular ensemble’s \\(\\chi\\) is not even the optimized one-use value. These three quantities—chosen ensemble information, optimized one-use information, and regularized capacity—must be named separately. Joint decoding may help even when the sender uses product codewords, so collective measurements and entangled encodings should not be conflated. Worked check 6.2. What does a code with 1024 messages and 100 channel uses transmit per use? Reasoned solution Its rate is log₂1024/100=0.1 bits per use. This is an achieved code rate only after its decoding error is specified; it is not by itself a capacity."}, {"title": "The erasure channel", "chapter": "Classical Communication over Quantum Channels", "url": "06-classical-communication.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 The erasure channel 6.3.1 A noise model with a readable flag A d-dimensional erasure channel transmits the input intact with probability \\(1-p\\) and outputs an orthogonal erasure flag with probability \\(p\\): \\(\\mathcal E_p(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho\\oplus p|e\\rangle\\langle e|\\). The flag lies outside the input’s signal space. It tells the receiver where a loss occurred, without telling them the missing message. This differs from replacing a qubit by I/2 within its ordinary output space. Use equally likely orthogonal signals. The output reveals the label when the signal survives and reveals none when the flag appears. The mutual information is \\((1-p)\\log_2d\\). The entropy calculation agrees: the average output entropy is \\(h_2(p)+(1-p)\\log_2d\\), while each signal output has entropy \\(h_2(p)\\). Their difference is the same rate. Classical erasure coding achieves the asymptotic unassisted capacity \\(C=(1-p)\\log_2d\\); the matching converse is a theorem, not established merely by evaluating this ensemble. See Bennett, DiVincenzo, and Smolin . For a qubit erased with probability 0.2, the classical capacity is 0.8 bits per use. The next chapter asks about preserving an unknown quantum state. The readable flag remains useful, but copying the surviving classical symbols is no longer an available repair strategy. Worked check 6.3. What is the classical capacity of a four-level erasure channel with p=0.25? Reasoned solution It is (1−0.25)log₂4=1.5 bits per use. The erasure flag does not add a message bit because its occurrence is independent of the chosen label."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-1", "text": "Write coherent information in terms of conditional entropy. \\(I_c=-S(R|B)=S(B)-S(RB)=S(B)-S(E)\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-2", "text": "What is Q for a noiseless qubit channel? One qubit per use; a maximally mixed input has coherent information one."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-3", "text": "Compute unassisted Q for p=0.3 qubit erasure. Q=1−2(0.3)=0.4 qubits per use."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute C, Q, and Q with two-way assistance for p=0.5 qubit erasure. C=0.5 bits/use, Q=0, and two-way assisted Q=0.5 qubits/use."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-5", "text": "Compute Q for a qutrit erasure channel at p=0.25. Q=0.5 log₂3≈0.792481 qubits per use."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-6", "text": "Explain why exact recovery of a reference entanglement forces decoupling. After a recovery isometry, RL is in the desired pure state and therefore factors from every remaining register. Tracing out local ancillas leaves R independent of E."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-7", "text": "A chosen input has coherent information −0.2. Does this imply negative capacity? No. Capacity is nonnegative, and pure inputs yield zero coherent information. Optimization and regularization may yield a positive rate."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-8", "text": "Can equal environmental marginals prove correctability of a code? No. Basis states can have equal marginals while superpositions reveal logical coherence to the environment. Correctability requires independence for every logical input, including a reference."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-9", "text": "Can the one-use coherent information always be called quantum capacity? No. It is an achievable-rate lower bound after optimization; equality needs additional conditions such as degradability."}, {"title": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#exercise-10", "text": "For p<1/2, find the extra erasure probability that simulates E from B. If extra erasure is q, then \\((1-p)(1-q)=p\\), giving \\(q=(1-2p)/(1-p)\\). This is a valid probability exactly in the stated range."}, {"title": "Coherent information", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Coherent information 7.1.1 Keep the reference in the calculation Purify an input \\(\\rho_A\\) as \\(|\\psi\\rangle_{RA}\\), apply an isometry \\(V:A\\to BE\\), and write the resulting pure state as \\(|\\omega\\rangle_{RBE}\\). The receiver holds B, while E is inaccessible. Define coherent information \\(I_c(\\rho,\\mathcal N)=S(B)-S(E)=-S(R|B)\\). The equality follows from purity: \\(S(E)=S(RB)\\). Unlike mutual information, coherent information may be negative. For the identity channel on a maximally mixed qubit, B has entropy one and E can be pure, giving \\(I_c=1\\). For a channel that replaces every input with a fixed pure state, B has entropy zero while E retains the input’s purification partner, so the same input gives \\(I_c=-1\\). The contrast captures the direction in which the input’s correlations have traveled. The quantum capacity Q is the supremum of asymptotic qubit transmission rates with entanglement-transmission error tending to zero, using independent channel uses and no assistance. Its coding theorem is \\(Q(\\mathcal N)=\\sup_{n\\ge1}n^{-1}\\max_{\\rho_{A^n}} I_c(\\rho_{A^n},\\mathcal N^{\\otimes n})\\). We state this theorem without its full proof; the general regularized expression appears in Watrous, Chapter 8 . Pure inputs ensure the maximized expression is at least zero, even when a particular mixed input gives a negative value. Worked check 7.1. What coherent information does a pure input give through any channel? Reasoned solution The output BE is pure when the input is pure, so S(B)=S(E) and coherent information is zero. This does not imply that the channel’s optimized coherent information is zero."}, {"title": "Decoupling", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Decoupling 7.2.1 Recovery is possible when the environment forgets Suppose the reference and environment are exactly independent: \\(\\omega_{RE}=\\omega_R\\otimes\\omega_E\\). Any purification of this product can be chosen as the desired entangled reference–logical state tensored with a purification of E. Since purifications of the same state differ by an isometry on the purifying system, an isometry on the receiver’s B system can recover that logical system, with an extra local ancilla if necessary. The environment has no record of the logical information, so its correlations can be restored at the receiver. This is the exact decoupling argument. Approximate product structure yields approximate recovery through continuity of purification, but turning it into coding rates requires quantitative estimates. A random encoder is not automatically a decoder, and small local noise alone is not a decoupling proof. The relevant comparison is the full joint RE state for the encoded source. There is also a converse intuition with an exact statement behind it. If B can perfectly recover an arbitrary logical state while preserving R, then the recovered RL pair is pure. A pure subsystem cannot remain correlated with the rest, so E must be independent of R. This is the information–disturbance connection underlying error correction. Chapter 8 makes the requirement algebraic for a code subspace. Worked check 7.2. Why is the marginal state of E alone insufficient to test decoupling? Reasoned solution Different joint RE states can have the same E marginal. A maximally entangled pair and the product of its marginals both have E=I/2, but only the product has no R–E correlation."}, {"title": "Erasure and assistance", "chapter": "Quantum Communication and Decoupling", "url": "07-quantum-communication.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 Erasure and assistance 7.3.1 The same channel has different operational capacities For a d-dimensional erasure channel, a maximally mixed input gives receiver entropy \\(h_2(p)+(1-p)\\log_2d\\). A complementary output receives the input on erased branches, giving entropy \\(h_2(p)+p\\log_2d\\). Subtracting yields \\(I_c=(1-2p)\\log_2d\\). The full unassisted quantum capacity is \\(Q=\\max\\{0,1-2p\\}\\log_2d\\). Its equality with this expression is a special theorem for the erasure channel, not a general license to remove regularization. For \\(p\\le1/2\\), the erasure channel is degradable: an additional erasure on B can reproduce the complementary channel. For \\(p\\ge1/2\\), the environment can simulate B; such antidegradable channels have zero unassisted quantum capacity, consistent with the no-cloning constraint. See the erasure-capacity paper for the capacity result and assistance distinctions. With unlimited two-way classical communication, the receiver can announce which halves of distributed Bell pairs survived. The surviving pairs support teleportation, and the erasure channel’s two-way assisted quantum capacity becomes \\((1-p)\\log_2d\\). For qubits with p=0.2, unassisted classical capacity is 0.8 bits/use, unassisted quantum capacity is 0.6 qubits/use, and two-way assisted quantum capacity is 0.8 qubits/use. The numbers refer to different tasks and cannot be interchanged. Worked check 7.3. At p=0.6, is the qubit erasure channel useless for distributing entanglement with classical feedback? Reasoned solution No. Unassisted Q is zero, but feedback identifies the surviving 40% of distributed Bell pairs. Two-way assistance achieves 0.4 ebits per use and supports that quantum transmission rate using teleportation."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-1", "text": "What does P represent in the correction condition? P is the orthogonal projector onto the code subspace; the condition is restricted to that subspace."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-2", "text": "List the X₂ syndrome in the repetition code. The syndrome is (−1,−1). Applying X₂ reverses the error."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-3", "text": "Evaluate the repetition-code logical error at p=0.01. The result is 0.000298 under independent bit flips and ideal encoding, syndrome extraction, and recovery."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-4", "text": "Why does Z₁ violate the condition for errors {I,Z₁}? PZ₁P has logical eigenvalues +1 and −1, so it is not proportional to P. The pair of errors is not correctable by this code."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-5", "text": "Show the Petz map for identity noise is identity on the reference support. For \\(\\mathcal N=\\operatorname{id}\\), the expression becomes \\(\\sigma^{1/2}\\sigma^{-1/2}X\\sigma^{-1/2}\\sigma^{1/2}=X\\) on the support."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-6", "text": "For complete dephasing and σ=I/2, find the Petz map. The scalar square roots cancel, so the recovery is dephasing itself. It recovers every diagonal state but cannot restore lost off-diagonal elements."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-7", "text": "If conditional mutual information is one bit, what fidelity guarantee remains? Some recovery achieves squared fidelity at least 1/2. The theorem does not promise near-perfect recovery in this case."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does correcting X₁ imply correcting every single-qubit operation? No. An arbitrary single-qubit operator has components I, X, Y, Z. A code must satisfy the criterion for their relevant span, including cross terms."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does the recovery inequality specify an efficient decoding algorithm? No. It guarantees a suitable channel exists. Efficient implementation and experimentally available operations require further analysis."}, {"title": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#exercise-10", "text": "Show the bit-flip repetition code improves the error probability for 0<p<1/2. \\(p-p_L=p-3p^2+2p^3=p(1-p)(1-2p)>0\\). This conclusion assumes ideal recovery and only independent bit flips."}, {"title": "Exact correction", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Exact correction 8.1.1 The Knill–Laflamme condition Let P project onto a code subspace and let \\(\\{E_a\\}\\) be errors spanning the noise operators of interest. Exact correction is possible precisely when \\(PE_a^\\dagger E_bP=c_{ab}P\\) for all a,b. The coefficient matrix is independent of the logical state. This is the Knill–Laflamme criterion . It covers the linear span of the specified errors, not arbitrary errors on an unspecified number of qubits. To see the necessity, use a dilation whose environment labels the errors. An environmental matrix entry is a linear combination of terms \\(\\langle\\psi|E_b^\\dagger E_a|\\psi\\rangle\\). If any such entry depends on the encoded logical input, the environment has learned logical information, contradicting the decoupling requirement for exact recovery. Requiring constancy for every superposition gives the operator condition on P, not just equality on selected basis states. For sufficiency, diagonalize the positive matrix c and replace the errors by the corresponding linear combinations. Their nonzero actions on the code become scaled isometries with mutually orthogonal images. A recovery measures which error-image subspace occurred and reverses its isometry. The syndrome distinguishes errors without distinguishing the logical state. Degenerate codes allow different physical errors to act identically on the code, so correcting noise does not require identifying a unique physical event. Worked check 8.1. Why must the criterion include a≠b, rather than only error probabilities Eₐ†Eₐ? Reasoned solution Off-diagonal products determine coherence between environmental error labels. Ignoring them can hide information leakage visible only for logical superpositions or coherent combinations of errors."}, {"title": "A limited but exact code", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 A limited but exact code 8.2.1 Bit flips and phase flips are different Encode \\(|0_L\\rangle=|000\\rangle\\) and \\(|1_L\\rangle=|111\\rangle\\). Consider the error set \\(\\{I,X_1,X_2,X_3\\}\\). Each error maps the code into a distinct two-dimensional subspace, and all four subspaces are orthogonal. Thus \\(PE_a^\\dagger E_bP=\\delta_{ab}P\\). Measuring the commuting parity observables \\(Z_1Z_2\\) and \\(Z_2Z_3\\) identifies which of the four subspaces occurred without measuring the logical amplitudes. The syndromes are (+,+) for I, (−,+) for X₁, (−,−) for X₂, and (+,−) for X₃. Applying the corresponding X reverses the error. If physical bit flips occur independently with probability p on each qubit, the majority decoder fails when two or three flips occur: \\(p_L=3p^2(1-p)+p^3=3p^2-2p^3\\). At p=0.1, this is 0.028. The code is exact for the zero-or-one-flip error family, but independent noise also includes uncorrectable multiple flips. A phase flip Z₁ acts as logical Z inside the code. Indeed, \\(PZ_1P\\) is not a scalar multiple of P. It changes the relative sign of the logical amplitudes while leaving both parity syndromes unchanged. Repeating computational-basis symbols therefore does not correct general single-qubit errors. The distinction is a reason to study richer codes, not a defect to conceal in the example. Worked check 8.2. What does Z₁ do to (|000⟩+|111⟩)/√2? Reasoned solution It gives (|000⟩−|111⟩)/√2, an orthogonal logical state. Both stabilizer parities remain +1, so the bit-flip recovery sees no error."}, {"title": "Recovering a family of states", "chapter": "Recovery and Quantum Error Correction", "url": "08-recovery-and-error-correction.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Recovering a family of states 8.3.1 Petz recovery and approximate Markov structure Recovery also appears in data processing. For a reference state \\(\\sigma\\) and channel \\(\\mathcal N\\), define the Petz map on the support of \\(\\mathcal N(\\sigma)\\) by \\[\\mathcal R_{\\sigma,\\mathcal N}(X)=\\sigma^{1/2}\\mathcal N^\\dagger\\!\\left(\\mathcal N(\\sigma)^{-1/2}X\\mathcal N(\\sigma)^{-1/2}\\right)\\sigma^{1/2}.\\] Inverses mean inverses on the support; an arbitrary channel extension handles the orthogonal complement. The map recovers \\(\\sigma\\). Under the usual support condition, equality in relative-entropy data processing for \\(\\rho,\\sigma\\) is equivalent to this map recovering \\(\\rho\\) too. We state this equality theorem rather than prove it; see the discussion of Petz’s theorem in Carlen and Vershynina, Recovery map stability for the Data Processing Inequality . It concerns recovery of a specified state pair, not a universal inverse of a noisy device. For a tripartite state, a related approximate theorem guarantees a channel \\(\\mathcal R_{B\\to BC}\\) such that \\(I(A:C|B)\\ge-\\log_2 F(\\rho_{ABC},(\\operatorname{id}_A\\otimes\\mathcal R)(\\rho_{AB}))\\), using our squared-fidelity convention. See Fawzi and Renner . This statement does not assert that the ordinary, unrotated Petz map always attains the bound. Small conditional mutual information therefore certifies existence of good recovery, while identifying an efficient recovery is an additional task. Worked check 8.3. If I(A:C|B)=0.1 bits, what squared fidelity does the recovery theorem guarantee? Reasoned solution At least 2^(−0.1)≈0.933033 for some recovery channel. This is an existence lower bound, not a claim that every reconstruction procedure achieves it."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-1", "text": "Name the free states in computational-basis coherence. All density matrices diagonal in that declared basis, including their probabilistic mixtures."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-2", "text": "What is Cᵣ(|+⟩)? Cᵣ=1−0=1 bit."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-3", "text": "What is Cᵣ(I/2)? Cᵣ=1−1=0."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute Cᵣ for √0.9|0⟩+√0.1|1⟩. Cᵣ=h₂(0.1)≈0.468996 bits."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-5", "text": "Show that dephasing is idempotent. \\(\\Delta(\\Delta\\rho)=\\Delta\\rho\\), because the first application removes every off-diagonal element and the second has nothing further to remove."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-6", "text": "Prove additivity of Cᵣ on product states. \\(\\Delta(\\rho\\otimes\\tau)=\\Delta\\rho\\otimes\\Delta\\tau\\); subtracting the additive input entropy from the additive dephased entropy gives \\(C_r(\\rho\\otimes\\tau)=C_r(\\rho)+C_r(\\tau)\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-7", "text": "Bound the number of exact |+⟩ outputs from ten copies of the r=0.6 state. m≤10(0.278072), so m≤2. This upper bound does not guarantee that two are achievable."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does one monotone inequality prove a state conversion exists? No. It gives a necessary condition. Sufficiency requires a theorem or an explicit allowed protocol."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-9", "text": "Do identical free-state sets force identical resource theories? No. Different operation classes can preserve the same free states while supporting different conversions and achievable rates."}, {"title": "Quantum Resource Theories — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#exercise-10", "text": "Explain why a catalyst’s unchanged marginal does not mean it was returned uncorrelated. Two copies of one fair classical bit each remain uniformly distributed, but their joint state is correlated rather than a product. Returning a catalyst marginal alone can hide correlations and differs from returning the original independent resource."}, {"title": "Choosing a resource theory", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 Choosing a resource theory 9.1.1 Free states alone are not enough A resource theory specifies a set of free states \\(\\mathcal F\\) and a collection of allowed channels that preserve this set. Identity and composition should be allowed; tensor-product rules must also be stated for multi-system tasks. A state outside \\(\\mathcal F\\) is a resource only relative to these restrictions. Unrestricted quantum operations could simply prepare the desired state, making the conversion problem trivial. For entanglement, free states are separable and a physically motivated operation class is LOCC. For basis coherence, free states are diagonal in a declared basis. Different operation classes can preserve those same diagonal states while permitting different transformations. Calling an operation “free” is therefore a substantive modeling choice, not a consequence of a preferred numerical measure. The broad framework is surveyed by Chitambar and Gour . Take a qubit whose free basis is |0⟩,|1⟩. The state |+⟩ has coherence, but |0⟩ does not. In the X eigenbasis, these roles change. This is not an inconsistency: the laboratory’s reference frame and permitted controls determine which basis has physical significance. For asymmetry under a symmetry group, free states are invariant and free channels are typically chosen covariant with that group; arbitrary basis dephasing is not a substitute for specifying the symmetry. Worked check 9.1. Is the Hadamard gate free in a resource theory where all diagonal states must remain diagonal? Reasoned solution No. It sends the free state |0⟩⟨0| to the coherent state |+⟩⟨+|. The preservation requirement already excludes it."}, {"title": "Relative-entropy monotones", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 Relative-entropy monotones 9.2.1 A general proof and a qubit calculation Define \\(R(\\rho)=\\inf_{\\sigma\\in\\mathcal F}D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)\\). If a channel \\(\\Lambda\\) maps every free state to a free state, then \\(R(\\Lambda\\rho)\\le D(\\Lambda\\rho\\|\\Lambda\\sigma)\\le D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)\\) for any free \\(\\sigma\\). Taking the infimum proves monotonicity. The first inequality uses free-state preservation; the second is data processing. This short proof shows exactly which physical assumption supports the bound. For coherence, write \\(\\Delta\\) for complete dephasing in the chosen basis. If \\(\\delta\\) is diagonal, then \\(\\tr\\rho\\log\\delta=\\tr\\Delta(\\rho)\\log\\delta\\). Adding and subtracting \\(S(\\Delta\\rho)\\) gives \\(D(\\rho\\|\\delta)=S(\\Delta\\rho)-S(\\rho)+D(\\Delta\\rho\\|\\delta)\\). Nonnegativity makes the optimum occur at \\(\\delta=\\Delta\\rho\\). Thus \\(C_r(\\rho)=S(\\Delta\\rho)-S(\\rho)\\). For \\(\\rho=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&r/2\\\\r/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\), with real \\(|r|\\le1\\), the eigenvalues are \\((1\\pm r)/2\\). Therefore \\(C_r=1-h_2((1+r)/2)\\). At r=0 the state is free and the value is zero; at r=1 it is |+⟩ and the value is one. The formula was derived for a fixed basis and should not be presented as basis independent. Worked check 9.2. Compute Cᵣ for r=0.6. Reasoned solution The eigenvalues are (0.8,0.2), so Cᵣ=1−h₂(0.2)≈0.278072 bits of relative-entropy coherence."}, {"title": "Conversion bounds and their limits", "chapter": "Quantum Resource Theories", "url": "09-resource-theories.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 Conversion bounds and their limits 9.3.1 Monotones constrain protocols If \\(\\rho\\) converts deterministically to \\(\\tau\\) by a free channel, every monotone obeys \\(R(\\rho)\\ge R(\\tau)\\). A violated inequality rules out the conversion. A satisfied inequality alone usually does not prove a protocol exists. Entanglement majorization provided a complete family of inequalities for a special pure-state problem; a single entropy loses part of that ordering information. If a monotone is additive, an exact conversion from n copies of \\(\\rho\\) to m copies of \\(\\tau\\) implies \\(nR(\\rho)\\ge mR(\\tau)\\). When the target value is positive, this yields \\(m/n\\le R(\\rho)/R(\\tau)\\). Relative-entropy coherence is additive because both entropy and dephasing factor on product inputs. Extending an exact bound to approximate asymptotic conversion requires an appropriate continuity argument; it does not follow just by deleting the word “exact.” Catalysts add another resource question: an auxiliary state participates and must be returned according to a stated criterion. Exact return as an uncorrelated product, approximate return, and returning only the same marginal are different requirements. A resource accounting that hides correlations with the catalyst can produce misleading conclusions. A research calculation should name its free operations, error tolerance, and return condition before optimizing a conversion rate. Worked check 9.3. What exact conversion bound follows from an input with Cᵣ≈0.278072 per copy to |+⟩ copies? Reasoned solution For free operations preserving diagonality, additivity and monotonicity require m/n≤0.278072. This is only a necessary condition; the calculation alone does not construct an exact distillation protocol."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-1", "text": "What is the optimal equal-prior success at θ=0? It is 1/2; there is no evidence to distinguish the labels."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-2", "text": "What is the optimal equal-prior success at θ=π/2? It is one, attained by the computational-basis measurement."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-3", "text": "Name one check on a numerical POVM. Check Hermiticity, nonnegative eigenvalues within tolerance, and that the effects sum to identity; checking only the objective is insufficient."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-4", "text": "Find the computational-basis success at θ=π/4. It is (1+1/2)/2=0.75, below the unrestricted value approximately 0.853553."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-5", "text": "For n=4,k=1,p=0.2 compute dimension and retained mass. D=1+4=5, requiring three qubits. The mass is 0.8⁴+4(0.2)(0.8³)=0.8192."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compute the resulting certified fidelity lower bound. 0.8192²=0.67108864. A three-qubit memory in this construction does not certify near-perfect fidelity."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-7", "text": "Derive the odd-parity phase-error probability for three independent phase flips. It is 3q(1−q)²+q³=3q−6q²+4q³. The bit-flip syndrome does not detect these logical phase flips."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-8", "text": "Do repeated optimizer runs prove global optimality? No. Multiple runs can find the same local solution. A matching dual certificate, analytic bound, or applicable global theorem is needed."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does an exhaustive parameter grid prove a continuous inequality? No. It is evidence on tested points. Rigorous interpolation bounds, interval methods, or an analytic argument are required to cover a continuum."}, {"title": "Research Practicum — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#exercise-10", "text": "Specify a reproducible extension of one project. For example, vary the prior in Project A. Record priors, angles, density matrices, numerical precision, and the constructed effects. Compare success against the weighted trace-norm formula and retain feasibility residuals. This is a verifiable extension without claiming a new theorem."}, {"title": "Project A: certify a measurement", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Project A: certify a measurement 10.1.1 A spectral benchmark before optimization Take the equally likely states \\(\\rho=|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\) and \\(\\sigma_\\theta=|\\psi_\\theta\\rangle\\langle\\psi_\\theta|\\), where \\(|\\psi_\\theta\\rangle=\\cos\\theta|0\\rangle+\\sin\\theta|1\\rangle\\), with \\(0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2\\). The analytic benchmark from Chapter 1 is \\(p_{\\rm guess}=(1+\\sin\\theta)/2\\). At zero angle the best guess is random; at a right angle it is perfect. A numerical routine that misses these endpoints is not ready for a harder instance. For each angle, diagonalize \\(\\rho-\\sigma_\\theta\\), construct the projector onto its positive eigenspace, and compute the success directly. Record Hermiticity, eigenvalue bounds, and completeness of the two effects. Then change the prior to 0.8 and use the weighted difference instead. The equal-prior formula is no longer the benchmark; the weighted trace-norm expression is. A useful extension restricts the receiver to computational-basis measurements. Compare its performance with the unrestricted optimum and identify where the gap is largest. Deliver the state definitions, parameter grid, precision, explicit effects at representative points, and residuals against the analytic result. These computations reproduce a known theorem and explore a constrained variant; they are not evidence of a new universal discrimination formula. Worked check 10.1. At θ=π/6 and equal priors, what values should the unrestricted and computational-basis tests produce? Reasoned solution The unrestricted optimum is (1+1/2)/2=0.75. Guess |0⟩ for outcome 0 and the other state for outcome 1; success is (1+sin²θ)/2=0.625."}, {"title": "Project B: finite-block memory", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Project B: finite-block memory 10.2.1 Compare a bound with an achieved construction Study the source with spectrum \\((1-p,p)\\), initially fixing p=0.2. For each block size n and cutoff k, retain the span of computational strings with at most k ones. Its dimension is \\(D_{n,k}=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\binom nj\\), and retained weight is \\(w_{n,k}=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\binom nj p^j(1-p)^{n-j}\\). Report memory \\(m=\\lceil\\log_2D_{n,k}\\rceil\\), rate m/n, and the certified entanglement-fidelity lower bound \\(w_{n,k}^2\\). At n=10,k=3, the benchmark is D=176, m=8, and w≈0.879126. To target fidelity at least 0.99 using this bound, choose the smallest cutoff with \\(w^2\\ge0.99\\). The condition is sufficient, not a proof that smaller memories cannot work. For p below one half, weight ordering matches individual-string probability ordering, but including entire weight shells may still use more dimension than a finer selection at a boundary. Vary n before varying p, so that changes in source entropy do not obscure finite-block effects. Plot the achieved sufficient rate against the entropy threshold. Describe the ceiling overhead and the confidence criterion. Extend to correlated binary sources only after specifying a transition model; replacing the binomial distribution without deriving a new source model would invalidate the calculation. Worked check 10.2. Why must a report label w² a lower bound instead of “the fidelity”? Reasoned solution The retained-subspace Kraus contribution gives w², while fallback branches can contribute additional overlap. The inequality certifies performance but does not generally determine the full channel’s exact fidelity."}, {"title": "Project C: specify the noise", "chapter": "Research Practicum", "url": "10-research-practicum.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 Project C: specify the noise 10.3.1 An honest bridge to a research note Use the repetition code as a controlled experiment. Under independent bit flips with probability p and ideal recovery, the logical error is \\(3p^2-2p^3\\). Verify the polynomial by enumerating the eight error patterns and comparing their decoded action on an arbitrary logical superposition. Then introduce independent phase noise with probability q on each physical qubit. Any odd number of Z errors causes logical Z, giving phase-error probability \\(3q(1-q)^2+q^3\\). This exposes a failure mode that a bit-flip-only benchmark hides. Choose one project and write a short research note: state the operational question, give a mathematical model, reproduce its benchmark, change one assumption, and explain the resulting limitation or conjecture. Supply equations, executable code, dependency versions, and enough intermediate data for another reader to reproduce the result. Include analytic endpoint checks and a separate discussion of floating-point tolerance. Assessment should reward a precise negative result as much as an attractive plot. A complete submission identifies resources and error criteria, reproduces a baseline within a stated tolerance, explains the modified model, and distinguishes proved statements from numerical observations. The supplied numerical verification script checks selected course examples using NumPy; it is a starting benchmark, not a replacement for your derivation. For further reading, return to the theorem references in the relevant chapter and follow their original proofs before proposing a generalization. Worked check 10.3. At p=q=0.01, compare the bit-flip logical error and the phase-flip logical error. Reasoned solution The bit-flip logical error is 0.000298. The phase error is 3(0.01)(0.99)²+(0.01)³=0.029404. Redundancy suppresses the modeled X errors while increasing exposure to uncorrected phase noise."}];
